<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!--
      For the two haptic ticks in the tab bar: one when a dragged screen arms
      over a slot, one when it lands there. A normal permission — granted at
      install with nothing to prompt for — and without it `navigator.vibrate`
      fails silently, so the gesture loses the only feedback it has at the
      moment the finger is covering the target.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />
    <!--
      The coach's one notification a day.

      The notification plugin's own manifest declares this too, so the merge
      would produce it either way — it is repeated here because this file is
      where someone looks to find out what the app asks the user for, and a
      permission that prompts should not be discoverable only by reading a
      dependency's AAR.

      Declaring it is half the job on Android 13 and up: it also has to be
      granted at runtime. That ask lives in `notify_today` rather than at
      launch, so the dialog appears the first time there is actually something
      to say. See the comment there.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!--
      Self-update. An installed app *is* allowed to hand the system a new APK
      of itself — what it can't do is install one silently, which needs to be a
      device owner. So the app downloads the new version and the system asks
      before replacing anything, which is the whole flow in `lib/updater.ts`.

      The safety here isn't this permission, it's the signature: Android
      refuses to install over an existing package unless the new one is signed
      with the same key. A malicious APK handed to `PackageInstaller` by this
      app would be offered as a *separate* install with its own package name,
      not as an update to this one.

      This is on Google Play's restricted list — only store-shaped apps may
      declare it — so it is stripped back out of the `.aab`. See the
      `playBundle` transform in `app/build.gradle.kts`, and RELEASING.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!-- AndroidTV support -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.software.leanback" android:required="false" />

    <!--
      Backups are off, and this is load-bearing rather than tidiness.

      The Garmin refresh token and the OpenRouter key live in this app's private
      directory (see `garmin-core::secrets` — Android has no keyring to put them
      in). Anything that copies that directory off the device copies both, and
      Android's automatic backup does exactly that by default: to Google Drive
      under `allowBackup`, and to a new handset under the device-to-device rules.

      Both are declared. `allowBackup` alone leaves D2D transfer enabled on
      Android 12 and up, and `dataExtractionRules` alone does nothing below it.

      The cost is that a new phone starts signed out. That is the correct trade
      for a credential this app can reissue with one sign-in.

      NOTE: this file is regenerated by `tauri android init`. If you ever re-run
      it, these two attributes and res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml come back
      out — check them before tagging a release.
    -->
    <application
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.app"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="${usesCleartextTraffic}">
        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:label="@string/main_activity_title"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
                <!-- AndroidTV support -->
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LEANBACK_LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <provider
          android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
          android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
          android:exported="false"
          android:grantUriPermissions="true">
          <meta-data
            android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
            android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>
    </application>
</manifest>
